2 Results
Overview of PhyE2E. PhyE2E comprises an end-to-end transformer-based physical model designed to take observed data points as input and predict both the operators and the physical units involved in a formula directly (Fig. 1). A set of 264,000 synthetic formulas were generated by an LLM (OpenLLaMA2-3B) that had been fine-tuned using real physics formulas from the Feynman Symbolic Regression Database (FSReD)[22] (Fig. 1 top). We randomly selected 180,000 formulas from the synthetic dataset for training, reserving the remaining data for testing. The inference process of PhyE2E involves three stages: a variable interaction detection method to decompose the target formula into sub-formulas (Fig. 1 bottom left), end-to-end prediction of each sub-formula using the trained model (Fig. 1 middle), and GA and MCTS approaches to refine the predicted formulas (Fig. 1 bottom right). We first fit the data using a standard multi-layer neural network, and used its Hessian matrix to determine the nonlinear interactions between all pairs of variables. The core of our model leverages a transformer architecture, synthesizing formulas as a prefix sequence with attached physical units. This approach incorporates both observed data points and physical prior knowledge about the target formula. In the final stage, we utilized GA and MCTS approaches to minimize the root-mean-square error (RMSE) of the predicted formula. This was achieved by using a grammar pool of context-free rules that incorporates basic operators like exp, as well as the most promising sub-formulas, which were automatically constructed within the search tree by PhyE2E.
Performance on the synthetic and AI Feynman datasets. We divided the synthetic dataset containing 264,000 formulas into training, validation, and test sets with a ratio of 80%, 10%, and 10%. We ensure that all validation and test formulas are unseen during training by removing formulas that are either identical, or become completely equivalent to the formulas in the training set after simple mathematical transformations (Sec. 4.5 in Methods). We compared PhyE2E with 13 state-of-theart symbolic regression baseline models, including 4 GA-based models (PySR[23], GP-GOMEA[5], Operon[24], and GPLearn[6]), 3 LLM-based models (TPSR[21], EndToEnd[19], NeSymReS[20]), and 6 NN-based models (AIFeynman[22, 25], uDSR[26], PhySR[27], ParFam[28], KAN[29, 30], BSR[31]). The technical details of running these models are provided in Supplementary Notes 3. We also included two variants of PhyE2E in our comparison, including versions without using the formula decomposition module (D&C) and the MCTS refinement module (MCTS). We evaluated the performance of all the models on 6 metrics[32] including symbolic accuracy, average accuracy(R2 > 0.99), unit accuracy, complexity, relative complexity to the ground truth formulas and elapsed times (Sec. 4.6 in Methods). First, we evaluated whether the physical formulas synthesized by the LLM were consistent with the real physical formulas from the Feynman Symbolic Regression Database (FSReD). It can be observed that the formulas generated by the LLM closely match the distribution of real
