4.3 The end-to-end model
Architecture. Kamienny et al.[19] established a transformer-based end-to-end model for symbolic regression. We designed a similar transformer that includes 4 encoder layers and 16 decoder layers, forming an asymmetric architecture[58]. Each layer consists of 16 attention heads and an embedding dimension of 512. We removed positional embeddings to ensure permutation invariance of the input data. Physical priors (e.g., physics units) play an important role in physical formulas[27, 59]. We integrated physical hints, including the physical units for both input and output variables, formula complexity, possible operators and possible constants, into the input of our model for predictions. Note that, the input of our model is a set containing the evaluations at N data points and h possible physics hints. Each data point was encoded into (n + 1) × 3 tokens, where each of the n features and the evaluation was encoded into 3 tokens, according to its sign, mantissa (between 0 and 9999) and exponent (from E-100 to E+100)[58]. Each of the h physical hints was encoded into specialized tokens. These encoded token strings were padded with the <pad> token so that each data point and each hint span exactly L = 33 tokens. They are finally concatenated together to form the input of our model. As for the output of our model, we additionally incorporated the physical units of each operator within the formulas as output sequence, making it compatible for the model to automatically infer dimensions of the target formula. While a formula can be encoded as a prefix expression consisting of operators and variables, we enhance it by associating physical units to each operator, providing intrinsic insights into the structure of physics formulas. Specifically, our model generates a sequence of (operator/variable, physical unit) as output. We utilize five of the most commonly used physical units as our bases, sightly different from the International System of Units (SI), including the Meter (m), Second (s), Kilogram (kg), Kelvin (K), and Volt (V)[22]. In this way, a physical unit can be encoded into five tokens. For dimensionless formulas, we simply assign each base unit with value 0.
Training details. The training data consisted of the data evaluated by 200,000 synthetic physical formulas generated in Sec. 4.1. Each formula was evaluated at N = 200 points sampled from the standard multivariate Gaussian distribution, conditioned on the fact that the formula is properly defined at the point, which follows the previous work[19]. We used the cross-entropy loss on the next-token prediction with the Adam optimizer, starting with a learning rate of 10−7, gradually increased to 2 × 10−4 during the first 10,000 steps, and
